{"name":"Gold Plate","hex":"#e6bd8f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gold plate","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A thin layer of gold applied to the surface of an object, often by an electrolytic method.","Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see gold,‎ plate."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To apply gold plate to an object, to plate with gold.","Of a project, to incorporate costly or otherwise excessive features or refinements unnecessarily, to overengineer."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold_plate"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gold plating","blocks":[{"text":"Gold plating is a method of depositing a thin layer of gold onto the surface of another metal, most often copper or silver, by a chemical or electrochemical (electroplating) process. Plating refers to modern coating methods, such as the ones used in the electronics industry, whereas gilding is the decorative covering of an object with gold, which typically involve more traditional methods and much larger objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/The_Sounds_of_Earth_Record_Cover_-_GPN-2000-001978.jpg/330px-The_Sounds_of_Earth_Record_Cover_-_GPN-2000-001978.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_plating","wikidata":"Q3391704"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Stirling_Engine.jpg/500px-Stirling_Engine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Stirling_Engine.jpg/500px-Stirling_Engine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A gold plated desktop Stirling engine","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStirling_Engine.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/National_Automobile_Museum%2C_Reno%2C_Nevada_%2823212336632%29.jpg/500px-National_Automobile_Museum%2C_Reno%2C_Nevada_%2823212336632%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/National_Automobile_Museum%2C_Reno%2C_Nevada_%2823212336632%29.jpg/1280px-National_Automobile_Museum%2C_Reno%2C_Nevada_%2823212336632%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/National_Automobile_Museum%2C_Reno%2C_Nevada_%2823212336632%29.jpg/500px-National_Automobile_Museum%2C_Reno%2C_Nevada_%2823212336632%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A gold plated DMC DeLorean—one of five known examples to have been plated—on display at the National Automobile Museum in Reno, Nevada","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANational_Au